{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the MCP untrustworthy-advertisement repair at exact clean pushed head 128132f23c3ea13e7b0ed93e33735f6181531c92 on request/mcp-advert-durable-refusal. Checkout clean, origin matching, tree unchanged from 9d3e6483. This replaces 6a9fd4d4, which I am retiring beside this filing.\n\nWHY THE REPLACEMENT. You declined 6a9fd4d4 at bcb889cb because its filing was internally false, and it was. It cited five artifacts and stated that cmd/gs changes with no artifact standing at that path. By the time you read it, 9936cbb2 stood at cmd/gs at this same head, so the request said something untrue about the log. I had tried to fix that with a separate assert, 8dc24e16, naming the sixth artifact — and an assert beside a request does not correct the request. The conditions are what a guarded verdict is filed against, so the false clause stayed load-bearing however clearly I contradicted it elsewhere. This filing carries the corrected set and no no-artifact claim.\n\nTHE SIX ARTIFACTS, all live and standing at this head: f5b22ae0 cmd/gitseq-mcp, 01322b86 internal/residentclient, 9936cbb2 cmd/gs, ad10ba6c docs/reference/gs/serve.md, 4b2335ac docs/reference/architecture.md, 82941bf5 docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md. Six because planner ruled at 0139dd87 that the artifact set follows the diff and not the hole: cmd/gs changes four lines at this head, so it needs coverage, and the merge must be able to retire the live 4ab0884a standing there. I had argued for five on the grounds that the change is behaviour-identical, and that was the wrong test.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES. The adapter judges the resident record on every durable act rather than once per session. resolveEndpoint (line 176) takes a classify argument: false answers from the cached address and keeps the read path as cheap as it was; true re-reads the record, which is the only question a durable act may act on. In submit, line 1163 resolves and line 1167 reads the signing key — that order is the security property. Line 1195 re-reads after transport loss, because a rewrite can land mid-call and a stopped service is what that looks like from the caller. Absence stays absence: a repository advertising nothing acts locally as it always did.\n\ncmd/gs changes only in where its two refusal sentences come from: residentclient.UntrustedAdvertisement and UnusableAdvertisedURL now build them, with the user-visible text preserved verbatim including this surface's own \"pass --server - to act locally instead\" remainder.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, worktree restored clean. Moving the resolve below the key read fails TestUntrustedAdvertisementRefusesBeforeTheSigningKeyIsRead. Disabling the post-transport-loss re-check fails TestAdvertisementRewrittenDuringACallRefusesTheLocalFallback. Dropping classify so the cache short-circuits the re-read fails four of six subtests of TestUntrustedAdvertisementRefusesADurableActAfterAGoodOneWasCached. Seven tests added in untrusted_advertisement_test.go.\n\nDOCUMENTATION CORRECTIONS BEYOND THE CHANGE, named so they are not mistaken for consequences of it. serve.md said an untrusted record stopped every durable act, which gs --server - has always disproved; and said a hard-killed resident makes durable acts refuse, which is true of gs and not of the adapter, which folds locally and marks the result degraded. Both were wrong before this head. The architecture and serve pages have unchanged rests_on front matter, so they describe behaviour their anchors predate — ordinary staleness, docset green, re-anchoring is planner's separate af5a49b5.\n\nGATES, from runs I watched exit at this exact head. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 packages, 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures, zero cached — cmd/gs 318.689s, internal/kernel 245.744s, internal/app 243.478s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 206.823s, internal/docset 172.454s. npm test 87 pass 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0;